Quoth Greg Sumner on this day of Tue, 13 Apr 2004 16:59:13 -0700:

>[Monks] are my new favorite class.  I've ascended most of the "easy" classes
>pretty easily, I would say with about 30% success rate on the fighter
>classes.  So I've played about 3 serious games and all 3 have died to due
>the difficulties inherent in the class.  That is mostly not having ability
>to wear armor or wield a weapon.  (Drowned by eel at Medusa's, blasted by
>disintegration in the castle, and I forget what happened to the 3rd)

I don't see how these deaths are specifically due to Monk
restrictions.

>Actually I was granted Vorpal Blade but I find having Grand Master martial
>arts is just as effective, at least for the mid-game.  Also I'm going for
>vegetarian conduct and wearing no metal.  Besides who ever saw a monk with a
>big long sword?

There we part company. I began using weapons once I was granted
Snickersnee, and ended up using Frost Brand through much of the game.
Incidentally, I ended up having to fight Master Kaen, and beat him to
death with Frost Brand on the stairs; it wasn't has hard as people
have made it out to be. 

I kept to vegetarian conduct (except for mistakes with a black pudding
and a wraith), and noted that many of the resistances are obtainable
through vegetarian corpses, before you would get them through
levelling up. I understand wearing no body armor (and did that
myself); I've never heard of wearing no metal. You may be restricting
yourself too much due to self-imposed conducts.
>
>So, what is your strategy?  I have read the Monk FAQ but I'd be interested
>to see what people have tried.  Mine has been to try the protection racket,
>but basic martial arts usually kills small creatures just by walking into
>them in a dark hallway.  

I don't see the point in using the protection racket for Monks. They
gain much more than they lose by levelling up, especially in the early
game.

>My other is to collect as many pets as possible
>until I can find something to wear.  

Collecting lots of pets is always good.

>It's harder than you would think to
>find good armor when you can only wear boots, gloves and leather helms.  

That's your own restriction. Nobody but you is stopping you from
wearing metal, as well as cloaks and hawaiian or T-shirts. Rings of
protection are also good.

>I'm
>loathe to give up my robe since I love the spell advantage, but I'll
>probably have to trade eventually for a magic-resistance cloak.
>
I'd keep the spell advantage, myself. The Eyes offer MR, and my monk
wished for Magicbane to use in a pinch.
